course: history
lesson: 64
title: Learning through the ages: schools, universities, libraries and science
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ryan moans about homework, so his teacher shows him a story from Sumer, about 4,000 years old, where a schoolboy gets punished for messy work and his dad has to sweeten the teacher. Ryan says «mood», slang for «same here, I feel that».
explain: Maya's friend says the Library of Alexandria burned in one night and all ancient knowledge was lost. What really happened: it was damaged by fire in 48 BCE and probably faded slowly over centuries as money and scholars left. Historians still argue about the details.
explain: Yasmin says the oldest university is in Fez, Morocco; Tom says Oxford. Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez was founded in 859 by a woman, Fatima al-Fihri, long before Oxford; but who is «first» depends on what you count as a university, so it is a real argument.
explain: Think of your library card: free books for anyone. For most of history that was a dream. Most children worked on farms or in workshops, many girls got no school at all, and most people learned a trade by watching and doing, like Patrick's plumber uncle.
explain: Zoe's teacher says science means being ready to be wrong, and Zoe finds it lowkey weird, meaning a bit strange. But that is the trick: test an idea, and change it if the test says no.
fact: In Sumer about 4,000 years ago, boys trained as scribes in «tablet houses», copying signs again and again; most children did not go to school.
fact: The Library of Alexandria in Egypt was founded around 300 BCE, was damaged by a fire in 48 BCE and probably declined slowly over centuries; the details are argued about.
fact: Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez was founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri; Bologna in Italy began around 1088 and Oxford later; Nalanda in India was a great centre of learning long before; who was «first» is contested.
fact: For most of history most children worked; laws making school required for all came in many countries in the 1800s and 1900s, and about 250 million children are still out of school today.
fact: For centuries many girls got little or no schooling; today nearly as many girls as boys go to primary school, but in Afghanistan girls have been banned from secondary school since 2021.
fact: In France, Diderot and others published the Encyclopédie from 1751 to collect knowledge for ordinary readers; Wikipedia, started in 2001, is written by volunteers and free.
fact: In Britain a law of 1850 let towns fund free public libraries, and Andrew Carnegie paid for about 2,500 library buildings around 1900.
fact: For centuries most people learned jobs as apprentices, living and working with a master for years, while guilds of bakers, weavers and builders set the rules.
fact: Medieval universities trained mostly priests, lawyers and doctors; they used Latin and words from guilds: a «master» could teach, like a master craftsman, and students could travel between countries.
fact: Ibn al-Haytham in Cairo, around the year 1000, tested ideas about light with experiments; science is a way of checking ideas, not just a list of facts.
exercise: About how old is the oldest known school story? | About 4,000 years | About 400 years | About 40 years | About 40,000 years
exercise: Where did boys in Sumer train as scribes? | In tablet houses | In royal castles | In big factories | On sailing ships
exercise: What really happened to the Library of Alexandria? | It was damaged by fire and probably declined slowly | It burned in one single night with every book inside | It was never built; the whole story is a legend | It is still open today, with all its old scrolls
exercise: Who founded Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez? | Fatima al-Fihri | Andrew Carnegie | Denis Diderot | King Hammurabi
exercise: Which university began in Italy around 1088? | Bologna | Oxford, in England | Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez | Harvard in America
exercise: Why is the «oldest university» question argued about? | It depends on what you count as a university | Nobody has ever been able to visit the old ones | All the universities opened on the very same day | The answer is a secret kept by the oldest one
exercise: When did laws making school required for all children come in many countries? | In the 1800s and 1900s | About 4,000 years ago, in Sumer | Only in the year 2020 | Back in the Stone Age
exercise: About how many children are still out of school today? | About 250 million | None at all | About 25 | Every child in the world
exercise: Where have girls been banned from secondary school since 2021? | Afghanistan | Iceland, in the far north | New Zealand and its islands | Canada, in some provinces
exercise: What was the Encyclopédie? | A French collection of knowledge from 1751 | A Roman law carved on a tall black stone | A medieval guild of book printers in Paris | An Egyptian library that burned in one night
exercise: Who writes Wikipedia? | Volunteers | Only paid kings | One single professor | A robot built in 1751
exercise: What did Andrew Carnegie pay for? | About 2,500 library buildings | The very first university in Europe | The rebuilding of the Library of Alexandria | The printing of the Encyclopédie
exercise: How did most people learn a job for centuries? | As apprentices with a master | At university | From the internet | From television
exercise: What did a «master» at a medieval university mean? | Someone allowed to teach, like a master craftsman | Someone who owned the university and its land | A famous sportsman who won the town games | A king's soldier who guarded the students
exercise: Why could medieval students travel between countries? | All the universities used Latin | They all spoke English at home | Borders did not exist in those days | They used little books to translate
exercise: What did Ibn al-Haytham do around the year 1000? | He tested ideas about light with experiments | He invented the first printing press for books | He burned the Library of Alexandria by mistake | He founded the University of Oxford in England
test: Ryan reckons school's a modern torture invented last century. What would his teacher say? | Schools are about 4,000 years old, but most kids did not go | Correct, school only began in about the year 2000 | Schools began with the internet, so about 30 years ago | Schools are old, but back then only grown-ups went to school
test: Maya's mate: «alexandria burned in ONE night, everything gone». Fair? | Too simple; it declined slowly and historians still argue | Totally right, one night, every scroll gone, done | It burned twice a year for a hundred years | Nah, it was flooded by the Nile and all the scrolls got wet
test: Tom says it's Oxford, no way it's Fez. What is the fairest answer? | Fez is much older, but «first» depends on what you count | Oxford is clearly older than Fez, by several hundred years | Neither of them has ever had a real university | Fez was only founded in 2001, so Oxford wins
test: What would happen if every library charged for each book? | Poor families could not read books they cannot buy | Everyone would read more, because paying is fun | Books would all become free in the shops instead | Nothing at all would change for anyone
test: Grandma Rosa's grandmother worked on the farm from age seven. Why was that normal then? | Most children worked; school for all came only recently | Schools were banned for everyone, children and grown-ups alike | Children liked farm work more than they liked lessons | There were no schools at all anywhere in the world then
test: Hamid says it's well unfair that girls were kept out of school. What is true today? | It was fought for, and gaps still remain in some places | Every girl everywhere now goes to school, no gaps left | Girls were never kept out of school anywhere at all | Only boys are allowed to go to school today
test: Lily's mum calls the Encyclopédie «Wikipedia from the 1750s». What is alike? | Both aim to share knowledge with ordinary readers | Both were written by machines, not by people | Both were banned by every king the moment they began | Both are carved on stone for the readers to visit
test: Patrick's uncle learned plumbing by watching and doing, bud. What old way is that? | An apprenticeship | A university degree, mate | A proper online course | A guild run by kings
test: In your strategy game, a university gives +1 tech. In real medieval Europe, what did universities mostly train? | Priests, lawyers and doctors | Plumbers, bakers and builders | Pilots and engineers | Footballers, actors and singers
test: Zoe says being ready to be wrong is lowkey weird for science. Why is it the point? | Testing and correcting ideas finds better answers | Scientists never change their minds once they decide | Science is just a long list of facts to learn by heart | Being wrong is never useful, so it should be hidden
exam: Identify the person credited with the foundation of Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in the year 859. | Fatima al-Fihri | Denis Diderot | Andrew Carnegie | Ibn al-Haytham
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the decline of the Library of Alexandria. | It suffered damage by fire and probably declined gradually over centuries | It was destroyed entirely in a single night, together with every scroll it held | It has remained in continuous operation since its foundation | It was never constructed and is known solely from legend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises apprenticeship as practised for centuries? | A learner resided and laboured with a master craftsperson for some years | A learner attended university lectures delivered in Latin for some years | A learner consulted printed encyclopaedias in a public library | A learner was forbidden to practise any trade until the age of thirty
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of the scientific method as practised by Ibn al-Haytham. | The testing of ideas by experiment and their revision | The memorisation of a fixed list of facts | The acceptance of all ancient opinions | The prohibition of all questions
